Z||000000||saleenfiero||03-24-2001||01:28 AM||||Maybe it is just my habit of always starting big ol' carbed motors, but I've noticed that my fiero 2.8 cranks, but won't fire without a little bit of gas.  Sometimes it will crank after 5 or 6 seconds on the key, but then it will lug at really low RPM until it smoothes out.  <P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||2
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||03-24-2001||02:00 AM||||Mine does that too, maybe it needs air. I think my IAC valve is messed up. Go about setting the idle like it says in the repair books. Does the book even explain it? I have 2 Haynes manuals but am too lazy to look. Maybe tomorrow, not that I'm promising anything.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||03-24-2001||02:10 AM||||Your cold start injector may not be working properly.......that would cause hard starting. <P>Another possibility is a dirty engine (internally).  You might have a build-up of carbon inside the engine causing hard starts and/or poor idle when cold. <P>Your regular injectors may be leaking too or the fuel pressure regulator may be faulty.<P>I had an '87 GT that was hard starting and idled funny.  I sold it to a guy as-is and he replaced the regulator.  He says it has been fine ever since.<P>Hopefully it's something simple.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||03-24-2001||06:34 AM||||I found that when my pick up module is getting worn out that it takes longer for my V6 to start. I don't have to touch the gas pedal to start but if the spark is not strong enough I would think it might have a little bit of a tough time to ignite the extra fuel the cold injector is putting in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LZeitgeist||03-24-2001||07:25 AM||||You could also change your fuel filter... it's six bucks (or so), and it's much easier than I thought it would be (at least on the '88's... I'm assuming that it's in the same place on other years...)<P>Even if it's not directly causing your problem, it still couldn't hurt... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||03-24-2001||09:05 AM||||My car with only 40,000 miles on it does the same thing , I was told to try the fuel pump relay... My oil pressure gauge builds and builds..gets up there ..then the car fires<P>any ideas?<BR>JM<BR>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||03-24-2001||12:08 PM||||Well, do you hear the fuel pump when you first turn on the key?  If not, check the fuel pump relay.  I'm still trying to figure out why my Formula won't run.  Changed the fuel filter, and it appears to be getting enough fuel pressue, and spark, but it won't start.  If I spray starting fluid into the intake, it fires.  Maybe an ECM problem and it isn't firing the injectors?||||reg||6
Z||000007||bubbajoexx||03-25-2001||12:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>My car with only 40,000 miles on it does the same thing , I was told to try the fuel pump relay... My oil pressure gauge builds and builds..gets up there ..then the car fires<P>any ideas?<BR>JM<BR>86 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>change the fuel pump relay the reason the engine takes so long to start is you are building oil presure to start the fuel pump the relay is for cold start up then the oil switch takes over to keep the pump on<BR>||||reg||1
